  "raw": {
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1360": {
      "title": "System Administrator",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States We’re hiring a System Administrator to design, administer, and secure the Saint Mary’s College digital campus. If you thrive on keeping critical systems running smoothly, modernizing the technical environment, solving complex infrastructure challenges, and building seamless integrations, this is your opportunity to make a direct impact on our campus community in a full-time, year-round position at our growing liberal arts institution. Join our collaborative, team-oriented IT department that focuses on continuous improvement and delivers high-visibility, transformational solutions for students, faculty, and staff throughout Saint Mary’s College. About the Role As our System Administrator, you’ll take ownership of our server and identity environments—designing, administering, and securing our Windows and Linux server and identity ecosystems. Reporting to the Director of Digital Infrastructure and Support Services, you’ll serve as a vital technical lead driving system uptime, identity management, automation, and cybersecurity. What You’ll Do Manage Core Server & Virtualization Infrastructure: Administer and upgrade Linux (Red Hat/CentOS/Debian) and Windows servers, enterprise VMware environments, and SAN storage systems (iSCSI/Fibre Channel), and design appropriate solutions that leverage cloud infrastructure. Drive Security & Compliance: Fortify security by implementing NIST and PCI standards and best practices, managing SSL certificates, and strengthening enterprise disaster recovery protocols. Lead Identity Management & SSO Integrations: Serve as the primary Active Directory, Azure, and Google Workspace administrator, as well as manage the identity solutions that provide SSO, LDAP, and MFA to secure access for students, faculty, and staff. Automate & Integrate Systems: Develop and maintain configuration and automation that leverages existing Python, Bash, SQL, and/or PowerShell scripts and modernizes those solutions to streamline operations and connect essential platforms like Banner, Blackboard, PaperCut, and others. Oversee Backup Architecture and Testing: Ensure that the College has appropriate backups and regularly recovery testing. Manage Technical Documentation : Develop and maintain technical runbooks, architecture diagrams, and system documentation. Support Campus Community: Collaborate across departments to troubleshoot complex system issues and deliver reliable technical services to a diverse academic community. What We’re Looking For Minimum Qualifications: Education/Experience: Bachelor’s degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, or a related field (equivalent extensive work experience fully considered in lieu of a degree). Server Administration: 3+ years of hands-on experience managing both Linux and Windows Server environments. Virtualization & Storage: 3+ years managing VMware or similar virtual infrastructure and SAN storage (iSCSI/Fibre Channel). Cybersecurity: Proven experience managing infrastructure to meet NIST and PCI security standards, maintaining enterprise backup and recovery solutions, managing SSL certificates, and using other security solutions to protect the enterprise environment. Database Support : Knowledge of Oracle and MySQL/MariaDB database systems. Scripting Skills: Proven ability to automate workflows, including through scripting using Python, Bash, Perl, SQL, or related languages. Identity & Access Management: Solid background in identity and access provisioning, Active Directory, LDAP, and SSO/MFA implementations. Support Experience: 3+ years in user-facing technical support, with strong communication and cross-functional collaboration skills. Teamwork & Communication : Excellent written, verbal, organizational, and interpersonal communication skills, ability to work effectively with a diverse group of individuals, and ability to adapt to changing priorities in a dynamic environment. environment. Preferred Qualifications: Previous experience in higher education IT environments. Experience with enterprise disaster recovery management. Experience with academic platforms (Banner, Blackboard, PaperCut). Deep understanding of NIST/PCI security standards and cloud/SSO enterprise integrations. Eligibility to Work: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1350": {
      "title": "Grant and Restricted Fund Accountant",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States Under the direction of the Controller, the Grant Accounting and Restricted Fund Accountant provides financial management and compliance oversight for grants, contracts, and other awards from foundation, corporate, and government sponsors supporting faculty research, scholarship, creative activities, and curricular, programmatic, and College initiatives. Description Under the direction of the Controller, the Grant Accounting and Restricted Fund Accountant provides financial management and compliance oversight for grants, contracts, and other awards from foundation, corporate, and government sponsors supporting faculty research, scholarship, creative activities, and curricular, programmatic, and College initiatives. The Grant Accounting and Restricted Fund Accountant works closely with Grant Managers, Principal Investigators (PIs), and others responsible for institutional and faculty-led grants. This position has primary responsibility for establishing accounting procedures and protocols, costing and allocations, monthly reporting and auditing of grants, and assisting with establishing grant budgets within the College’s financial systems. The Grant Accounting and Restricted Fund Accountant is responsible for monitoring and properly recording indirect funds related to grants in compliance with the Office of Management and Budget’s Uniform Guidance. This position monitors grant funds from award setup through closeout while adhering to College, State, and Federal policies and requirements. This position plays an important role in post-award compliance by ensuring that grant budgets are not overspent, grant periods are followed, costs are allowable under the terms of the grant, and accounting records are accurate, complete, and maintained appropriately. The Grant Accounting and Restricted Fund Accountant also prepares and analyzes monthly reports for endowed and other restricted funds to ensure spending does not exceed allowable amounts. This position works closely with the Senior Financial Planning Analyst to budget for restricted positions and known expenditures. Supervision Received Reports to the Controller. Works closely with the Vice President of Finance and Operations, Controller, Vice Presidents and Directors, Grant Managers and Principal Investigators, and the Office of Academic Research, Grants and Sponsored Programs. Supervision Exercised This position has no direct reports but provides joint oversight with the Controller of accounting staff working on grant and restricted fund accounting. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in accounting, Finance, or a related field. Five or more years of experience in accounting, internal controls, and/or auditing. Experience with grants and contracts administration, preferably within a higher education setting. Experience with federal Uniform Guidance requirements. Experience with endowed and restricted fund accounting. Extensive knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite, Google applications, and accounting software. Strong problem-solving skills with an understanding of data analysis and forecasting. Highly organized and detail-oriented, with the ability to work independently and collaboratively. Ability to handle highly confidential information responsibly. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, particularly when working with individuals without a financial background. Strong data-entry skills with a high degree of accuracy. Ability to accurately prepare and maintain records, files, reports, and correspondence. Demonstrated ability to complete assignments accurately and with attention to detail. Ability to analyze, organize, and prioritize work effectively while working under pressure and meeting deadlines. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated strong financial and quantitative skills. Excellent written and verbal communication and presentation skills. Strong interpersonal and collaboration skills and the ability to build effective working relationships. Demonstrated ability to work independently and manage projects effectively. Strong leadership and relationship-building skills. Ability to learn quickly and apply strong problem-solving and critical-thinking skills. Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office, particularly Microsoft Excel. Experience developing reports from ERP systems using tools such as SmartView or Tableau. Experience with grant management systems such as Kyuse and Oracle Cloud. Experience with grant accounting, post-award administration, and compliance in a higher education environment. Essential Job Functions Ensure compliance with federal, state, and College regulations, policies, and procedures. Research, interpret, and apply sponsor regulations and guidelines to ensure grant and financial compliance. Develop policies and procedures and document internal controls related to financial compliance. Track and maintain records required for state and federal compliance, including audit reports, procurement documentation, compensation certifications, and risk assessments. Assist with and/or perform grant-related post-award functions, including award setup, budget distributions, expense monitoring, periodic invoicing, and financial reporting. Review and approve labor distribution transfers and related communications, reconciliations, and re-budgeting activities. Prepare journal entries to allocate residuals, overdrafts, and/or overhead discrepancies to departmental accounts resulting from reconciliations and grant closeouts. Maintain regular communication with departments and fiscal contacts at funding sources to resolve issues and gather information. Perform grant closeout activities, including reconciliations, final invoices, and final reporting. Manage grant-related data, prepare financial reports, and lead monthly grant financial review meetings. Review and approve grant expenditures to ensure costs are allocable, allowable, and reasonable. Track and report on cost-share commitments. Assist with budget revisions throughout the grant period. Collaborate with the Finance Office during grant and College audits. Assist the Finance Office with grant-related invoicing, subaward recipient risk assessments and monitoring, and award closeout. Assist in preparing and reviewing grant budgets and update the College Budget system to support accurate reporting of actual expenditures against the approved budget. Prepare monthly reports and analysis related to other restricted funds to identify potential overspending and notify the Accounting Office of concerns. Communicate with departments regarding activity and balances associated with restricted funds. Prepare journal entries to correct discrepancies related to restricted fund activities. Perform other duties as assigned. Schedule Full-time, Monday through Friday, during regular College business hours. The position may require occasional additional hours to meet reporting, audit, grant closeout, or other critical deadlines. Work Authorization Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from Saint Mary’s College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1351": {
      "title": "Accountant (1 Year Limited Term)",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States Saint Mary’s College is seeking a detail-oriented and service-focused Staff Accountant to join the Finance team for a one-year limited-term appointment. Under the direction of the Assistant Controller, the Staff Accountant will support the day-to-day accounting operations of the College by preparing accounting worksheets, performing account analysis, assisting with monthly closing activities, and providing general support to the Finance team. Description Saint Mary’s College is seeking a detail-oriented and service-focused Staff Accountant to join the Finance team for a one-year limited-term appointment. Under the direction of the Assistant Controller, the Staff Accountant will support the day-to-day accounting operations of the College by preparing accounting worksheets, performing account analysis, assisting with monthly closing activities, and providing general support to the Finance team. The Staff Accountant will work collaboratively with members of the Finance team and budget managers across the College to ensure financial information is accurate, timely, and properly recorded. This position provides an excellent opportunity for an accounting or finance professional to contribute to the College’s financial operations while working in a collaborative and service-oriented environment. The successful candidate will be organized, detail-oriented, dependable, and comfortable managing multiple priorities while maintaining a high level of accuracy and professionalism. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree from an accredited college or university with a focus in Accounting, Finance, or a related field. Three to five years of related accounting or finance experience, or recent college graduate with relevant education and experience. Demonstrated ability to independently manage ongoing projects, programs, or assignments. Working knowledge of Microsoft Excel and Word. Strong attention to detail and commitment to accuracy. Ability to prepare accounting worksheets, reports, and other financial information accurately and ensure amounts reconcile to appropriate source documentation. Ability to communicate effectively, both verbally and in writing. Ability to establish and maintain positive working relationships with students, faculty, staff, and members of the public. Ability to provide professional and courteous customer service and project a positive image of Saint Mary’s College. Ability to organize and prioritize work while completing assignments accurately and in a timely manner. Preferred Qualifications Previous experience in a higher education, nonprofit, or institutional finance environment. Experience assisting with monthly financial close processes and preparation of journal entries. Experience performing account reconciliations and analysis and identifying potential adjusting journal entries. Experience working with budget managers to review and reallocate expenses. Strong Microsoft Excel skills, including experience working with financial data and spreadsheets. Demonstrated ability to learn new financial systems, processes, and procedures. Ability to work independently while also contributing effectively as part of a collaborative Finance team. Strong organizational, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Demonstrated commitment to providing responsive, professional, and service-oriented support to colleagues across the College. Essential Job Functions Assist the Finance team with monthly closing activities, including preparation and entry of journal entries. Perform account analysis and identify and propose appropriate adjusting journal entries. Prepare and maintain accounting worksheets, reports, reconciliations, and related financial documentation. Work with budget managers to review and facilitate the reallocation of expenses as needed. Assist with various accounting and clerical functions in support of the Finance team. Respond to telephone inquiries in a courteous and professional manner, answer questions when appropriate, direct calls to the appropriate individual, or take accurate messages. Maintain accurate and organized financial records and documentation. Complete assigned tasks, projects, and special assignments accurately and within established timelines. Provide professional and courteous service to students, faculty, staff, and members of the public. Perform other duties and projects as assigned. Schedule Full-time, Monday through Friday, during regular College business hours. Contract Term: This is a one-year limited-term position . The appointment will conclude at the end of the one-year term unless otherwise extended by the College. Work Authorization Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from Saint Mary’s College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1349": {
      "title": "General Services Technician",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States Saint Mary’s College is seeking a dependable and hands-on General Services Technician to join our General Services team. This position plays an important role in supporting campus events, daily operations, and the overall appearance and functionality of College facilities. Saint Mary’s College is seeking a dependable and hands-on General Services Technician to join our General Services team. This position plays an important role in supporting campus events, daily operations, and the overall appearance and functionality of College facilities. The General Services Technician provides event setup and tear-down support, including the delivery, setup, relocation, and storage of tables, chairs, risers, flags, floor coverings, pipe and drape, audio/visual equipment, and other event-related materials. The position also assists with office moves, furniture relocation, large deliveries, recycling collection, and stocking of departmental supplies throughout campus. The position will also assist with custodial responsibilities and respond to custodial needs and emergencies as assigned. The successful candidate will be comfortable working in a fast-paced, physically demanding environment and will take pride in providing excellent service to students, faculty, staff, and campus guests. This position will work closely with the General Services Supervisor and may provide direction to student workers during event setup and tear-down activities. The ability to read and follow work orders and room diagrams, organize work efficiently, meet deadlines, and work effectively as part of a team is essential. Responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Set up and tear down tables, chairs, risers, flags, pipe and drape, floor tarps, and other event equipment. Assist with audio/visual setups under the direction of the General Services Supervisor. Transport, deliver, unload, set up, and store equipment and supplies for campus events. Operate College vehicles for on-campus and off-campus deliveries. Perform office moves, including relocating furniture, records, supplies, and equipment. Deliver and stock departmental and central supply materials. Deliver large copy paper orders and other large shipments. Assist with unloading and transporting large deliveries throughout campus. Complete daily recycling pickups. Assist with custodial work and respond to custodial emergencies as needed. Operate custodial equipment and other equipment necessary to perform assigned duties. Attend weekly event meetings and follow event safety procedures. Provide direction and assistance to student workers during event setup and tear-down. Work assigned weekends as part of a rotating schedule. Provide courteous and professional customer service to the campus community. Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent. Three (3) years of special event, facilities, general services, or related experience. Valid driver’s license. Ability to operate heavy cleaning equipment. Ability to operate a forklift; annual training will be provided. Ability to read and understand work orders and room diagrams. Strong organizational skills and the ability to prioritize and complete work efficiently. Ability to work effectively under pressure and meet deadlines. Strong interpersonal and customer service skills. Ability to perform physically demanding work, including walking, lifting, climbing, crawling, pushing, pulling, and carrying. Ability to regularly lift and carry items weighing up to 75 pounds. Ability to routinely assemble and lift objects weighing up to 30 pounds overhead. Ability to work outdoors in varying weather conditions, including heat, rain, sleet, and snow. Preferred Qualifications Experience with stage assembly and event setup. Knowledge of event and technical safety regulations. Experience using hand and power tools. Experience with custodial equipment and general facility operations. Forklift operation training or certification. Knowledge of workplace safety practices, including Blood-Borne Pathogens, Personal Protective Equipment (PPE), and Ladder Safety. Previous experience working in a college, university, event, facilities, or general services environment. Schedule This is a full-time position with a schedule that may vary based on campus events and operational needs. The position will participate in a rotating weekend schedule and may require occasional evening, weekend, or extended hours to support College events and activities. The General Services Technician must be flexible and available to work outdoors and during varying weather conditions as needed. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1345": {
      "title": "Custodian, 3rd Shift",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Custodian is responsible for cleaning campus buildings including: mopping, stripping and/or finishing floors, cleaning areas requiring use of ladder, operating heavy cleaning equipment, rodent and pest control and moving furniture or classroom building materials The Custodian is responsible for cleaning campus buildings including: mopping, stripping and/or finishing floors, cleaning areas requiring use of ladder, operating heavy cleaning equipment, rodent and pest control and moving furniture or classroom building materials The above is intended to describe the general content of and requirements for the performance of this job. It is not to be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities and requirements. Minimum Qualifications Ability to climb ladders to reach high places including replacing ceiling light bulbs, move heavy furniture, lift heavy boxes (50 – 75 pounds), operate heavy cleaning equipment, and ability to mop extensive area. Possess organizational skills and ability to work well with others. Preferred Qualifications Prior related job experience (1 – 2 years). Training or prior experience in use of cleaning equipment and job functions. Eligibility to Work: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. Schedule 40 Hours/ 12 Months/ Monday - Friday "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1346": {
      "title": "Custodian, 3rd Shift",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Custodian is responsible for cleaning campus buildings including: mopping, stripping and/or finishing floors, cleaning areas requiring use of ladder, operating heavy cleaning equipment, rodent and pest control and moving furniture or classroom building materials The Custodian is responsible for cleaning campus buildings including: mopping, stripping and/or finishing floors, cleaning areas requiring use of ladder, operating heavy cleaning equipment, rodent and pest control and moving furniture or classroom building materials The above is intended to describe the general content of and requirements for the performance of this job. It is not to be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities and requirements. Minimum Qualifications Ability to climb ladders to reach high places including replacing ceiling light bulbs, move heavy furniture, lift heavy boxes (50 – 75 pounds), operate heavy cleaning equipment, and ability to mop extensive area. Possess organizational skills and ability to work well with others. Preferred Qualifications Prior related job experience (1 – 2 years). Training or prior experience in use of cleaning equipment and job functions. Eligibility to Work: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. Schedule 40 Hours/ 12 Months/ Monday - Friday "
