    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1341": {
      "title": "Maintenance Mechanic",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Maintenance Mechanic will have primary responsibility for the College’s plumbing systems and related plumbing maintenance and repairs. We are seeking a candidate with a strong background and high level of skill in plumbing, including the ability to troubleshoot, diagnose, repair, maintain, and install plumbing systems and fixtures. The Maintenance Mechanic will have primary responsibility for the College’s plumbing systems and related plumbing maintenance and repairs. We are seeking a candidate with a strong background and high level of skill in plumbing, including the ability to troubleshoot, diagnose, repair, maintain, and install plumbing systems and fixtures. The successful candidate should be comfortable working independently and handling a wide range of routine and complex plumbing issues across campus.is responsible for troubleshooting, installing, inspecting, operating, maintaining, and repairing mechanical systems and equipment in support of the College's facilities and maintenance operations. This position works across multiple trades and provides support for mechanical equipment, plumbing, electrical systems, fire alarm and suppression systems, emergency generating equipment, and other campus infrastructure. The Maintenance Mechanic works collaboratively with other members of the maintenance team to identify and resolve equipment issues, perform preventive maintenance, maintain accurate records, and ensure work areas and mechanical spaces are safe, clean, and organized. The position requires sound judgment, problem-solving skills, technical knowledge, and the ability to work independently as well as part of a cross-functional team. The Maintenance Mechanic works under the direct supervision of the Director of Maintenance and may direct the work of assigned helpers or student employees as needed. Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent. Minimum of four (4) years of related maintenance or mechanical work experience. Valid driver's license. Knowledge of mechanical equipment and systems. Knowledge of fire alarm and suppression systems. Knowledge of plumbing systems and equipment. Knowledge of electrical equipment and controls. Basic computer and email skills. Working knowledge of Microsoft Office, including Excel. Ability to safely use tools, equipment, and appropriate personal protective equipment. Ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a team. Preferred Qualifications Experience working in a college, university, institutional, commercial, or similar facilities environment. Experience with computerized maintenance management systems (CMMS), preferably SchoolDude. Experience with preventive maintenance programs. Strong troubleshooting and mechanical problem-solving skills. Ability to establish and maintain positive working relationships with students, faculty, staff, and colleagues. Ability to communicate effectively and provide assistance and technical guidance to fellow employees. Experience reading and interpreting blueprints, schematics, service manuals, and technical documentation. Job Responsibilities Perform mechanical repairs and maintenance as directed or required. Troubleshoot mechanical devices and equipment, including pumps, engines, motors, conveyor systems, and related equipment. Observe equipment in operation and use sound, visual, and other diagnostic methods to identify malfunctions. Dismantle equipment to access defective components, replace or repair parts, and reassemble equipment for proper operation. Lubricate, clean, adjust, and maintain mechanical equipment and components. Install, adjust, and maintain equipment and functional components to ensure proper operation. Perform maintenance and repairs on emergency generating equipment, electrical control systems, fire alarm systems, lighting, construction-related equipment, and plumbing fixtures and equipment. Perform basic plumbing repairs within the scope of assigned responsibilities. Conduct routine rounds of mechanical rooms and other assigned areas to identify maintenance needs, including lighting, ceiling tiles, floor tiles, and other facility concerns. Support other maintenance trades with repair, installation, and maintenance activities as needed. Report significant equipment problems or repairs requiring additional expertise or resources to the Director of Maintenance. Plan and maintain an inventory of parts, supplies, and materials required for assigned maintenance and repair responsibilities. Estimate quantities and costs of materials needed to complete assigned work. Maintain service manuals, maintenance logs, records, blueprints, schematics, and other technical documentation. Perform assigned preventive maintenance activities. Utilize the College's CMMS, SchoolDude, to receive, document, update, and complete assigned work orders. Follow established procedures for ordering materials and supplies. Utilize computers to prepare reports and documentation as directed by the Director of Maintenance. Maintain work areas, shops, tools, workstations, and mechanical spaces in a safe, clean, and organized manner. Follow established safety procedures and use all required personal protective equipment. Assist and provide technical guidance to fellow maintenance staff in a supportive and professional manner. Serve as a resource and instructor to other staff members when appropriate. Work as part of a cross-functional maintenance team and perform duties outside the traditional scope of the Maintenance Mechanic position when operational needs require. Maintain compliance with applicable governing and regulatory requirements within areas of responsibility. May direct the work of assigned helpers or student employees. Perform other duties as assigned. Schedule Full-time, 12-month position. This position may require working evenings, weekends, holidays, emergency call-ins, and other hours as necessary to support the College's facilities and operations. The position requires the ability to walk, crawl, climb stairs and ladders, stoop, bend, and lift for extended periods. The employee must be able to work in confined areas, operate vehicles, and lift and carry up to 70 pounds. Eligibility to Work Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1342": {
      "title": "Campus Minister for Justice and Solidarity",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Campus Minister for Justice and Solidarity leads student formation and co-curricular learning focused on justice, solidarity, service, and community engagement. The Campus Minister for Justice and Solidarity leads student formation and co-curricular learning focused on justice, solidarity, service, and community engagement. This position integrates Catholic social teaching and the spirituality and charism of the Sisters of the Holy Cross into service and justice programming while creating opportunities that welcome and engage students from diverse faith backgrounds. The Campus Minister oversees the Beyond the Belle and Spes Service Experience programs, including program development, logistics, student recruitment and preparation, reflection and integration, and student leader development. The position supervises student community engagement leaders, Spes Service Experience leaders, and interns involved in justice and advocacy programming. The Campus Minister collaborates across campus to strengthen experiential learning opportunities and develops and maintains meaningful relationships with local community organizations and ministry partners. This position also provides ongoing mentorship and pastoral care to students and contributes to initiatives that advance the mission of Saint Mary’s College as a Catholic and Holy Cross institution. Minimum Qualifications Master of Divinity, Theology, Spirituality, or a related field required. Experience in higher education in a student-facing role or experience working in ministry with young adults. Experience planning and leading service opportunities and justice-focused programming. Knowledge of and commitment to Catholic social teaching. Demonstrated ability to develop and execute programs or events from conception through completion. Proficiency with computer applications, including Google Workspace. Commitment to hospitality and welcome and the ability to engage students from diverse faith backgrounds. Excellent written, verbal, public speaking, interpersonal, and social communication skills. Demonstrated ability to build effective relationships and collaborate with students, faculty, staff, community partners, and the Sisters of the Holy Cross. Ability to work independently, manage multiple responsibilities, and collaborate effectively as part of a team. Preferred Qualifications Experience planning and leading justice immersions or service immersion experiences. Familiarity with the charism and spirituality of the Sisters of the Holy Cross. Experience developing student leadership and formation programs. Experience working with community organizations, nonprofit agencies, or ministry partners. Experience integrating faith formation, justice, service, and experiential learning in a higher education setting. Job Responsibilities Lead the Beyond the Belle and Spes Service Experience programs, including program development, logistics, recruitment and preparation of student participants and faculty/staff advisors, reflection and integration meetings, and student leader development. Collaborate with the Prikkel Advising, Career, and Experience Center, faculty, and other campus partners to strengthen experiential learning opportunities for students. Help students connect faith, justice, and service through guided preparation and reflection that incorporates Catholic social teaching and the charism of the Sisters of the Holy Cross. Oversee and guide the direction of Holy Cross Justice Circles in collaboration with the Director of the Holy Cross Justice Collaborative. Advise the Catholic Relief Services (CRS) Ambassadors program and maintain communication with the local CRS Coalition. Oversee Federal Work-Study Community Service job placements, hiring, and timecard approval in collaboration with Student Financial Services. Develop and maintain relationships with local organizations, community partners, and ministry organizations. Supervise and mentor student community engagement leaders, Spes Service Experience leaders, and student interns who lead justice and advocacy programming. Provide ongoing mentorship, pastoral support, and guidance to students. Evaluate programs and recommend opportunities for growth, improvement, and increased student engagement. Collaborate with the Center for Faith, Action and Ministry team on strategic planning, student leader formation, special events, and other initiatives that advance the mission of Saint Mary’s College. Contribute to a welcoming and inclusive campus environment by developing programs and opportunities that encourage students of diverse backgrounds and faith traditions to participate in service, justice, and community engagement. Perform other duties and responsibilities as assigned. Schedule This is a full-time, 12-month position working approximately 40 hours per week. Due to the nature of student programming, the position requires periodic evening and weekend hours as assigned. The position also requires periodic domestic and/or international travel associated with service immersion experiences. Eligibility to Work Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. Please submit a current resume and cover letter with your application. Your cover letter should highlight your relevant experience, qualifications, and interest in the position. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1340": {
      "title": "Lead Learning & Instructional Design Specialist",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Lead Learning Systems & Instructional Design Specialist reports to the Director of Instructional Design and Technology and occasionally serves in the Director's absence as needed. The Lead Learning Systems & Instructional Design Specialist reports to the Director of Instructional Design and Technology and occasionally serves in the Director's absence as needed. This full-time, 12-month position works closely with the Director to provide research-based support and training in instructional technology, instructional design, accessibility, and learning management systems. The position supports the effective use of instructional technology across the College, partners with faculty and staff to develop engaging and accessible learning experiences, and assists with the maintenance and operation of classroom and online instructional technologies. Minimum Qualifications Master's degree in Instructional Design, Educational Technology, or a related field. Experience in IT systems administration, learning management system (LMS) management, or web development, with demonstrated expertise managing learning technologies, system integrations, or web platforms, preferably in a higher education environment. At least three years of experience in teaching and/or curriculum development, including experience with online, hybrid, and face-to-face instruction. Experience developing and facilitating instructor-led and self-paced training, preferably in virtual environments. Experience developing training and professional development programs for diverse audiences. Advanced knowledge of instructional design principles, Universal Design for Learning (UDL), and best practices in curriculum and training design. Working knowledge of ADA and WCAG accessibility standards and demonstrated ability to create accessible instructional materials and remediate inaccessible documents. Demonstrated technical proficiency with one or more LMS platforms, Microsoft Office, and Google Workspace. Experience providing end-user technical support and troubleshooting LMS and classroom instructional technology issues. Strong interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Strong organizational, project management, and time-management skills, with the ability to manage multiple projects and priorities simultaneously. Demonstrated problem-solving and troubleshooting skills with strong attention to detail. Experience with online meeting and collaboration platforms such as Kaltura Classroom, Google Meet, Microsoft Teams, Zoom, or similar technologies. Working knowledge of the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in teaching and learning. Commitment to accessibility, inclusion, and equitable practices in educational technology. Preferred Qualifications Experience administering Blackboard Ultra. Experience with LTI 1.3 integrations. Experience working with APIs and data synchronization between an LMS and other internal or external platforms. Knowledge of IT security practices and applicable regulations, including FERPA, GDPR, and HIPAA. Experience maintaining or enforcing security policies designed to protect data and systems. Familiarity with HTML. Experience with Kaltura or other video management platforms. International Association of Accessibility Professionals (IAAP) certification, such as CPACC or ADS. Experience supporting instructional technology and classroom technology systems, including Crestron. Experience with recording studios, lightboards, multimedia systems, or related instructional technology. Job Responsibilities The Lead Learning Systems & Instructional Design Specialist reports to the Director of Instructional Design and Technology and occasionally serves in the Director's absence as needed. This full-time, 12-month position works closely with the Director to provide research-based support and training in instructional technology, instructional design, accessibility, and learning management systems. The position supports the effective use of instructional technology across the College, partners with faculty and staff to develop engaging and accessible learning experiences and assists with the maintenance and operation of classroom and online instructional technologies. Instructional Design Partner with faculty to design and develop engaging, effective online and hybrid courses aligned with the College's academic goals and recognized standards for high-quality online learning. Consult with faculty on instructional design, pedagogy, and the effective use of educational technologies to enhance student learning. Support faculty in designing and developing instructional materials, videos, course modules, and other learning resources for online, hybrid, and face-to-face courses. Incorporate emerging technologies, including generative AI, into course design and pedagogy in thoughtful, effective, and appropriate ways. Support faculty in applying accessibility, inclusion, and Universal Design for Learning principles to course content, design, and delivery. Develop instructor-led and online training materials, including participant materials, facilitator guides, train-the-trainer materials, online training courses, videos, and web conferences for faculty, staff, and students. Research and evaluate emerging instructional technologies, tools, and best practices in higher education and recommend improvements based on institutional needs and student learning outcomes. Participate in ongoing professional development to remain current with instructional technology, LMS platforms, accessibility standards, AI, and higher education trends. Conduct training needs assessments and evaluate the effectiveness of training programs. Develop and recommend new training programs and improvements to existing programs based on identified needs and emerging technologies. Collaborate with faculty, staff, instructional designers, instructional technologists, IT personnel, and other stakeholders to develop learning solutions aligned with institutional goals. LMS Administration Share responsibility with the Director for administration, management, maintenance, and support of the College's LMS. Configure the LMS and manage user access, course information, and related systems. Provide technical support and troubleshooting for faculty, staff, and students experiencing LMS-related issues. Develop and maintain LMS documentation, resources, and user support materials. Create and monitor vendor helpdesk requests and coordinate with vendors to resolve technical issues in a timely manner. Collaborate with instructional designers, instructional technologists, IT staff, faculty, and other stakeholders to integrate tools and features that enhance teaching and learning. Collaborate with vendors and internal IT teams on system upgrades, integrations, and compatibility with existing and emerging technologies. Manage the integration of multimedia and interactive elements within the LMS. Generate, analyze, and distribute reports related to LMS usage, course completion, and other relevant metrics to support academic and operational decision-making. Assist with periodic reviews of the LMS to ensure compliance with institutional policies, data security standards, accessibility requirements, and applicable regulations, including ADA, WCAG, and FERPA. Maintain the confidentiality and security of student records and other protected information. Assist with the implementation and evaluation of new LMS features, tools, integrations, and functionality. Instructional Technology Assist with training and supervising student workers who provide classroom hardware troubleshooting and support for Crestron systems. Collaborate with ITRC staff to diagnose and resolve instructional technology hardware issues. Assist with training users of the recording studio and support the maintenance of recording studio equipment, lightboards, and related technology. Maintain accurate records and documentation of instructional technology equipment located in academic spaces. Assist with ensuring classroom and online instructional technology is maintained and functioning properly. Perform other duties as assigned. Schedule This is a full-time, 12-month position. The regular schedule will be established based on the operational needs of the department and the College. Occasional evening, weekend, or extended hours may be required to support instructional technology, training, system implementations, or other College needs. Eligibility to Work Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. Please submit a current resume and cover letter with your application. Your cover letter should highlight your relevant experience, qualifications, and interest in the position. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1336": {
      "title": "Director Advancement Operations",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States Saint Mary’s College is seeking an experienced and strategic Director of Advancement Operations to lead the systems, data, processes, and operational infrastructure that support the College’s fundraising efforts. Saint Mary’s College is seeking an experienced and strategic Director of Advancement Operations to lead the systems, data, processes, and operational infrastructure that support the College’s fundraising efforts. Reporting to the Vice President for Advancement, the Director will provide leadership for Advancement Services and Prospect Research and will play a key role in ensuring the Division has the tools, information, and processes needed to achieve its fundraising goals. This position will oversee the development and continuous improvement of Advancement systems and workflows, with a strong focus on data integrity, reporting, analytics, process optimization, and collaboration across departments. The Director will serve as a strategic partner to Advancement leadership and will help transform data into meaningful insights that support decision-making, donor engagement, and fundraising success. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree required. Minimum of five years of demonstrated experience in advancement operations, fundraising systems, data management, or a related management role; higher education experience preferred. Demonstrated experience leading teams responsible for data, reporting, CRM systems, prospect research, or related functions. Experience with process mapping, workflow optimization, and operational redesign. Strong knowledge of data governance, reporting, analytics, and financial reconciliation processes. Experience implementing, managing, or optimizing CRM/database systems. Strong organizational, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Excellent communication and collaboration skills, with the ability to work effectively with senior leaders and cross-functional teams. Demonstrated commitment to continuous improvement and operational excellence. Preferred Qualifications Experience working in a higher education advancement or fundraising environment. Experience with CRM/database migration projects and implementation of new systems. Experience developing dashboards, reporting tools, and business intelligence frameworks. Experience working with fundraising data, gift processing, donor stewardship, prospect research, and advancement services. Experience collaborating with Finance on gift processing, fund reconciliation, endowment reporting, and donor-restricted funds. Experience developing policies, procedures, training materials, and operational documentation. Experience providing leadership, mentorship, and professional development to staff. Job Responsibilities Lead the design, implementation, and continuous improvement of systems, processes, workflows, and reporting that support fundraising effectiveness. Develop and maintain an Advancement Dashboard and other reporting tools that support data-driven decision-making across the Division. Provide centralized data, analytics, reporting, and prospect research support for Advancement leadership and staff. Oversee Advancement Services and Prospect Research, providing strategic direction and operational leadership. Ensure the accuracy, integrity, security, and appropriate use of Advancement data and databases. Lead process mapping, system optimization, workflow redesign, and database migration initiatives, including collaboration with external vendors when appropriate. Ensure systems and processes effectively support stewardship activities, including gift acknowledgments, reporting, compliance, and donor communications. Establish and maintain effective information flow between Advancement and Finance related to gift processing, fund reconciliation, endowment reporting, and donor-restricted funds. Partner with the Executive Director of Annual Giving and Donor Relations and other Advancement leaders to align operational strategies with fundraising priorities. Participate as a key member of the Advancement Leadership Team, contributing to Division-wide planning, goal setting, and strategic initiatives. Design and implement processes that maximize the effective use of data in support of Advancement goals. Analyze Advancement data and trends and translate findings into meaningful reports and recommendations for leadership. Manage competing requests for reports, mailing lists, prospect research, biographies, and other Advancement information and resources. Support comprehensive campaign planning and execution through accurate data, reporting, research, and operational processes. Ensure compliance with fundraising policies, procedures, and established goals. Research, design, and implement new programs and processes that support the goals of the Advancement Division. Maintain and regularly update an Advancement Operations manual documenting policies, procedures, workflows, and best practices. Develop and maintain appropriate documentation, policies, procedures, and audit processes to promote consistency and operational effectiveness. Collaborate with Finance and program areas to ensure donor-restricted funds are managed and used in accordance with donor intent. Provide mentorship, guidance, and professional development opportunities for Advancement staff. Perform other duties as assigned in support of the College and Advancement Division. Schedule 40 hours per week / 12 months / Monday–Friday Eligibility to Work Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. Please submit a current resume and cover letter with your application. Your cover letter should highlight your relevant experience, qualifications, and interest in the position. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1337": {
      "title": "Prospect Research Analyst",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Prospect Research Analyst is a primarily research- and data-driven position responsible for analyzing and documenting information to assess financial capacity, philanthropic tendencies, giving propensity, and connections to Saint Mary’s College. The Prospect Research Analyst is a primarily research- and data-driven position responsible for analyzing and documenting information to assess financial capacity, philanthropic tendencies, giving propensity, and connections to Saint Mary’s College. The successful candidate will be a strong collaborator who is comfortable and skilled in using prospect research databases and is excited about gathering, analyzing, and synthesizing information. The Prospect Research Analyst will demonstrate a strong commitment to providing accurate, highly confidential, and ethical information. The ideal candidate will have a love of learning, a natural curiosity about data, strong attention to detail, and a focused and persistent approach to gathering information. The position requires an independent self-starter who can effectively manage multiple priorities while supporting the College’s fundraising goals. The position will conduct confidential prospect research reports and briefings, complete data-related tasks, monitor relevant current news and information about prospects, maintain data integrity, and collaborate closely with field fundraisers to support donor cultivation and solicitation strategies. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution. 2–5 years of experience in prospect research, fundraising research, data analysis, development, or a related field. Strong organizational skills and exceptional attention to detail, accuracy, and data integrity. Demonstrated ability to conduct research, analyze information, and break down complex problems using effective research and analytical techniques. Ability to gather, synthesize, and communicate large amounts of information from multiple sources in a clear and concise manner. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Proficiency with computerized systems, including Microsoft Word, Excel, Google Workspace (Sheets and Documents), and CRM databases. Ability to manage and prioritize multiple projects and meet deadlines effectively. Ability to exercise tact, discretion, and sound judgment when handling confidential and sensitive information. Strong commitment to providing accurate, complete, accessible, and timely information. Demonstrated ability to work independently as a self-starter while also collaborating effectively with colleagues and stakeholders. Preferred Qualifications Experience with prospect research within a higher education, nonprofit, or fundraising environment. Knowledge of general fundraising principles, practices, and the major gift fundraising process. Experience using prospect research and fundraising databases or platforms, including CRM systems. Knowledge of financial research and the ability to evaluate financial capacity, philanthropic history, giving propensity, and relationships or connections to an organization. Experience researching publicly available financial and corporate information, including SEC filings, press releases, corporate disclosures, and news sources. Experience preparing prospect research reports, donor briefings, and other materials to support fundraising and donor cultivation strategies. Familiarity with prospect management, portfolio development, list creation, data analysis, and fundraising reporting. Knowledge of current trends and best practices in prospect research and development. Experience supervising or mentoring student workers or other support staff. Job Responsibilities Identify and research prospective major gift donors by analyzing financial capacity, philanthropic tendencies, giving propensity, relationships, interests, and connections to Saint Mary’s College. Conduct comprehensive research and prepare confidential prospect research reports and briefings to support donor cultivation, engagement, and solicitation strategies. Monitor current news, publicly available financial information, corporate activity, and other relevant information related to alumni, friends, donors, and prospects. Identify and track significant accomplishments, career developments, life events, philanthropic activity, and areas of interest for alumni, friends, donors, and prospects. Enter and maintain accurate and timely prospect information in the College’s CRM, including employment, education, contact information, relationships, interests, and other relevant data. Maintain the integrity, accuracy, completeness, and accessibility of prospect and donor information. Prepare detailed research briefings and background materials for senior leadership and development staff to support donor cultivation and solicitation efforts. Partner with field fundraisers by providing timely and relevant research and information to assist with prospect strategy, engagement, cultivation, and solicitation. Assist the Director of Research for Development with prospect management, list creation, data analysis, reporting, and other development research initiatives. Collaborate with Gift Processing, Alumnae Relations, Development, and other College departments to support fundraising and advancement objectives. Stay current on prospect research trends, tools, resources, and best practices. Supervise and provide direction to one student worker. Perform other duties as assigned. Schedule This is a full-time position. The regular schedule is Monday through Friday during normal business hours, with occasional evening and weekend work required based on departmental needs, special events, or College activities. Eligibility to Work Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. Please submit a current resume and cover letter with your application. Your cover letter should highlight your relevant experience, qualifications, and interest in the position. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1335": {
      "title": "Custodian, 1st Shift",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Custodian is responsible for cleaning campus buildings including: mopping, stripping and/or finishing floors, cleaning areas requiring use of ladder, operating heavy cleaning equipment, rodent and pest control and moving furniture or classroom building materials. The Custodian is responsible for cleaning campus buildings including: mopping, stripping and/or finishing floors, cleaning areas requiring use of ladder, operating heavy cleaning equipment, rodent and pest control and moving furniture or classroom building materials The above is intended to describe the general content of and requirements for the performance of this job. It is not to be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities and requirements. Minimum Qualifications Ability to climb ladders to reach high places including replacing ceiling light bulbs, move heavy furniture, lift heavy boxes (50 – 75 pounds), operate heavy cleaning equipment, and ability to mop extensive area. Possess organizational skills and ability to work well with others. Preferred Qualifications Prior related job experience (1 – 2 years). Training or prior experience in use of cleaning equipment and job functions. Eligibility to Work: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. Schedule 40 Hours/ 12 Months/ Monday - Friday "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1316": {
      "title": "Regional Development Director -Remote",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States Saint Mary’s College is seeking a Regional Development Director - Remote to build meaningful, long-term relationships between benefactors and the College within a designated region of the United States. Saint Mary’s College is seeking a Regional Development Director - Remote to build meaningful, long-term relationships between benefactors and the College within a designated region of the United States. This position will support the strategic goals of Saint Mary’s to empower, enrich, and advance women in all stages of life through the power of philanthropy. As the only women’s college in Indiana and one of fewer than 30 women’s colleges in the country, Saint Mary’s offers a distinctive educational experience that combines a liberal arts education with graduate studies and research opportunities. The College prepares women for leadership in their careers and communities while fostering a campus experience rooted in faith, belonging, and purpose. Saint Mary’s is experiencing significant momentum through the Ring Out Ring True $200 million comprehensive campaign, centered on three enduring commitments: Empowering Leaders, Enriching Our Community, and Enhancing Our College. The Regional Development Director will play an important role in advancing these priorities through strategic philanthropy and donor engagement. The Regional Development Director will work closely with academic leaders and Development colleagues to identify, cultivate, solicit, and steward leadership-level philanthropic gifts of $25,000 or more. This position is remote, with a preference for candidates residing in the greater Chicagoland area or Michigan. Please submit a current resume and cover letter with your application. Your cover letter should highlight your relevant experience, qualifications, and interest in the position. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree required. Five or more years of fundraising experience with demonstrated success cultivating and soliciting gifts of $100,000 or more; or a proven track record of building relationships and closing complex, high-value agreements with decision-makers. Demonstrated excellent oral and written communication skills. Strong organizational, time management, and prioritization skills, with experience managing complex projects from concept through execution. Excellent critical listening and relationship-building skills. Proven ability to establish priorities and manage multiple initiatives simultaneously. Strong work ethic and a demonstrated record of achieving professional goals. Proficiency with Microsoft Office. Experience using a Constituent Relationship Management (CRM) system to manage donor relationships and workflow. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated success as a major gifts' fundraiser, preferably within higher education. Basic understanding of relevant tax and estate law and planned giving mechanisms. Experience promoting or supporting gift planning initiatives. Ability to live within a 3–4-hour driving distance of Saint Mary’s College. Preference for candidates residing in the greater Chicagoland area or Michigan. Job Responsibilities Implement the College’s fundraising strategy focused on the identification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of gifts of $25,000 or more. Manage a portfolio of approximately 150 high-level prospects and establish and achieve annual giving and major gift objectives. Develop and implement donor-centered philanthropic engagement plans that connect donors’ interests with the priorities and strategic needs of Saint Mary’s College. Build and maintain significant, long-term relationships with current and prospective benefactors whose philanthropic support is essential to advancing the College’s strategic priorities. Cultivate and solicit leadership-level gifts of $25,000 or more, including demonstrated success in securing gifts of $100,000 or more. Partner with academic leaders, Development colleagues, volunteers, and other College representatives to engage key prospects and donors. Promote the College’s gift planning program and identify opportunities for planned gifts. Prepare and edit written materials for prospective donors, the President, volunteers, and other key stakeholders to support high-level donor engagement. Travel extensively, up to 50%, within the assigned region and nationally as needed to cultivate and strengthen donor relationships. Maintain accurate and timely records of donor interactions, strategies, solicitations, and follow-up activities within the College’s CRM system. Work in partnership with the Executive Director of Regional Development and Gift Planning. Support the success of the Ring Out Ring True comprehensive campaign by advancing philanthropic relationships and securing support for the College’s strategic priorities. Schedule Full-time, 12-month position. This is a remote position with extensive travel, up to 50%, within the designated region and as needed to support donor engagement. Preference for candidates living in the greater Chicagoland area or Michigan. Eligibility to Work Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1287": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Donor Relations",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Associate Director of Donor Relations serves as a key member of the Annual Giving and Donor Relations team and the broader Advancement Division. This position is responsible for developing and executing a comprehensive, coordinated donor stewardship and recognition program for Saint Mary’s College. The Associate Director of Donor Relations serves as a key member of the Annual Giving and Donor Relations team and the broader Advancement Division. This position is responsible for developing and executing a comprehensive, coordinated donor stewardship and recognition program for Saint Mary’s College. This role ensures that donors at all levels experience timely, meaningful, and mission-centered engagement that reflects the impact of their generosity. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree. Three to five years or more of related professional experience, preferably in development, annual giving, donor relations, fundraising, or higher education advancement. Understanding of the donor lifecycle and principles of effective donor stewardship. Demonstrated ability to develop and execute comprehensive stewardship strategies. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to create compelling, donor-centered communications. Exceptional attention to detail and strong organizational skills. Ability to manage multiple projects, priorities, and deadlines. Ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues and campus partners. Ability to exercise sound ethical judgment and uphold donor intent. Commitment to the mission and values of Saint Mary’s College. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education advancement or a nonprofit development environment. Experience with donor stewardship, recognition, annual giving, or fundraising programs. Experience preparing endowment reports and donor impact communications. Experience managing donor recognition programs or giving societies. Experience working with donor databases, advancement systems, and reporting tools. Experience analyzing donor engagement, retention, and stewardship data. Strong storytelling, editing, and content development skills. Knowledge of current donor relations and stewardship best practices and trends in higher education advancement. Job Responsibilities Endowment & Impact Reporting Oversee the preparation and distribution of endowment reports and other fund-specific impact reports in collaboration with Finance, Advancement Services, and campus stakeholders. Ensure reports are accurate, clear, engaging, and tailored to donor interests. Communicate measurable outcomes and student impact through meaningful donor-centered storytelling. Evaluate and enhance reporting formats to improve readability, engagement, and effectiveness. Donor Stewardship Strategy & Execution Develop and implement a comprehensive donor stewardship program aligned with Saint Mary’s College’s mission, values, and fundraising priorities. Ensure consistent and coordinated stewardship practices across annual giving, major gifts, leadership giving, and endowed funds. Establish stewardship standards, timelines, and best practices. Ensure donor communications are accurate, timely, personalized, and meaningful. Develop strategies that encourage continued engagement and philanthropic support regardless of a donor’s giving level or type. Donor Recognition Lead college-wide donor recognition efforts, including leadership, lifetime, loyalty, and legacy giving societies. Coordinate special donor acknowledgments and recognition opportunities. Partner with Advancement Marketing & Communications to integrate donor recognition across print and digital platforms. Maintain thoughtful and inclusive recognition practices that reflect donor intent and institutional values. Foster a culture of philanthropy throughout the College. Data Assessment & Continuous Improvement Collaborate with Finance and campus partners to review fund usage and ensure alignment with donor intent. Work with Advancement Services to track stewardship activities, donor recognition status, and reporting compliance. Use donor feedback, engagement data, and retention metrics to evaluate the effectiveness of stewardship efforts. Recommend improvements based on data, donor feedback, and program outcomes. Stay current on donor stewardship best practices and trends in higher education advancement. Schedule Full-time, 12-month position. 40 hours per week Monday–Friday Eligibility to Work Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1285": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Graduate Admission",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Assistant Director of Graduate Admission is an on-campus position responsible for supporting recruitment, admission, marketing, communications, and event planning efforts for the Office of Graduate Admission. The Assistant Director will work closely with the Dean of Enrollment Management and Marketing, Director of Admission, graduate program directors, and marketing team to recruit and enroll talented graduate students in the College’s master’s and doctoral programs. Saint Mary’s College is a nationally recognized women’s Catholic college experiencing remarkable momentum, with growing enrollment, record philanthropic support, and a bold vision for the future. Through innovative initiatives like the Avenue Experience, we connect every student’s education to meaningful career preparation while investing in transformative experiences that extend far beyond the classroom. If you’re looking to make a lasting impact alongside talented colleagues who are shaping the next generation of women leaders, Saint Mary’s is an exceptional place to build your career. Please note that Saint Mary’s College is co-educational at the graduate level. The Assistant Director of Graduate Admission is an on-campus position responsible for supporting recruitment, admission, marketing, communications, and event planning efforts for the Office of Graduate Admission. The Assistant Director will work closely with the Dean of Enrollment Management and Marketing, Director of Admission, graduate program directors, and marketing team to recruit and enroll talented graduate students in the College’s master’s and doctoral programs. This position plays an important role in developing and implementing graduate recruitment strategies, managing admission communications, supporting the application and decision-making process, coordinating recruitment events, and strengthening relationships with prospective students and key campus constituents. The successful candidate will be an organized, creative, and collaborative professional with strong communication skills and the ability to build meaningful relationships with prospective graduate students and individuals from diverse backgrounds. Job Responsibilities Recruitment & Marketing Develop and implement effective marketing and recruitment campaigns to attract qualified graduate students. Create, write, edit, and coordinate email, print, digital, and other marketing communications for prospective graduate students and key influencers. Work with marketing vendors on direct mail, digital marketing, and electronic marketing initiatives. Create engaging and meaningful marketing and web content for graduate programs. Help maintain accurate, effective, and engaging graduate program webpages. Represent Saint Mary’s College professionally within the academic community and with prospective graduate students, families, alumni, professional organizations, faculty, and staff. Travel as needed to meet with prospective students and other key constituents and promote the College’s graduate programs. Stay informed about graduate enrollment trends, recruitment strategies, and emerging technologies. Administrative & Collaboration Manage the flow of information between the Office of Graduate Admission and graduate programs and departments. Collaborate with the Director of Admission and graduate program directors to manage the application review and decision notification process. Represent Graduate Admission on graduate program committees. Collaborate with graduate program directors and campus departments to develop strategies to recruit and retain graduate students. Utilize, interpret, and analyze enrollment and admission data to help determine the most effective use of resources. Contribute to an enrollment management and team-based approach to addressing graduate recruitment and enrollment challenges. Coordinate the implementation and effective use of emerging technologies related to graduate admission and recruitment. May provide direction to student employees working with the Office of Graduate Admission. Establish priorities, manage multiple projects, and meet deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Event Planning & Implementation Coordinate and manage campus visits and events for prospective graduate students. Assist with planning and implementation of annual graduate admission events. Support orientation programs, recruitment events, campus visits, and online webinars. Work collaboratively with campus partners to ensure a positive and welcoming experience for prospective graduate students. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree. Professional experience in college admission, recruitment, marketing, communications, or a related field. Recruitment experience in an admission office, preferably at the graduate level. Experience developing and implementing communication and marketing initiatives. Experience writing marketing emails and editing written communications and publications. Experience utilizing traditional and digital marketing tools. Excellent written, verbal, public speaking, interpersonal, and social communication skills. Ability to establish rapport and build effective relationships with individuals from diverse backgrounds. Ability to communicate effectively with prospective graduate students, applicants, alumni, faculty, students, and staff. Strong organizational and time-management skills, with the ability to establish priorities and manage multiple responsibilities. Ability to work independently and exercise sound reasoning and judgment. Ability to collaborate effectively as part of a team and direct the work of others when appropriate. Ability to analyze and interpret data and use information to support recruitment and enrollment strategies. Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree. Three or more years of experience in college admission, recruitment, marketing, or a related higher education setting, preferably graduate admission. Spanish proficiency. Experience with graduate admission and enrollment management. Demonstrated ability to provide leadership and develop programs. Schedule Full-time, on-campus position. Schedule will be determined based on the operational needs of the Office of Graduate Admission and may include occasional evening and weekend hours, as well as travel, to support recruitment events, campus visits, webinars, and other admission activities. Eligibility to Work: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1268": {
      "title": "Hall Director",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States This position provides an exceptional opportunity to positively impact students' college experience by fostering belonging, encouraging leadership, promoting student success, and supporting personal growth within a dynamic residential environment. Join a team dedicated to creating transformative residential experiences. Saint Mary's College is a nationally recognized Catholic, residential women's college rooted in the charism of the Sisters of the Holy Cross. We are committed to educating women to make a difference in the world through academic excellence, leadership development, and a vibrant residential community. The Residence Hall Director is a critical member of the Student Affairs team and serves as an educator, mentor, administrator, and community builder. Through intentional relationships, leadership development, and student support, Residence Hall Directors create welcoming living-learning communities where students thrive academically, socially, spiritually, and personally. This position provides an exceptional opportunity to positively impact students' college experience by fostering belonging, encouraging leadership, promoting student success, and supporting personal growth within a dynamic residential environment. This is a full-time, 12-month, live-in position Job Responsibilities Community Development Develop, implement, and assess educational initiatives, community traditions, and engagement opportunities that promote belonging, wellness, leadership development, civic responsibility, and student success. Create an inclusive residential environment where students feel welcomed, valued, respected, and connected. Build meaningful relationships with residents through regular interaction, mentorship, and visible leadership. Encourage student engagement through educational, social, and leadership programming aligned with the College's mission. Promote diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging while supporting the Catholic identity and values of Saint Mary's College. Address student conduct concerns through educational conversations, documentation, sanctions, referrals, and follow-up. Advocate for students by connecting them with campus resources and community partners. Student Development Support students experiencing academic, emotional, behavioral, or personal concerns through intervention, mentoring, and referrals. Collaborate with campus partners to promote student well-being, persistence, and academic success. Facilitate conflict resolution and mediation among residents. Engage students in meaningful conversations that foster personal responsibility and leadership. Assess residential learning outcomes and utilize assessment results to enhance future programming. Staff Leadership & Supervision Recruit, select, train, supervise, mentor, and evaluate 10–18 Resident Assistants and front desk student employees. Conduct weekly staff meetings and provide ongoing leadership development opportunities. Meet regularly with Resident Assistants to provide coaching, performance feedback, and professional development. Foster a collaborative, supportive, and accountable student staff team. Residence Hall Operations Respond to emergencies, crisis situations, student behavioral concerns, and campus incidents while exercising sound judgment and professionalism. Participate in an on-call rotation, including evenings, weekends, holidays, and overnight responsibilities. Maintain regular office hours and remain accessible to students. Coordinate room inspections, occupancy management, housing transitions, and work orders. Collaborate with Facilities and Campus Safety to maintain a safe and welcoming residential environment. Support departmental initiatives including room selection, staff recruitment, training, recognition programs, social media, committee work, and special projects. Attend departmental meetings and collaborate with campus partners to support divisional initiatives. Summer Responsibilities Serve as the primary professional staff member overseeing summer housing operations. Supervise summer student employees and residence hall operations. Support summer conference and camp housing programs. Assist with office operations and coverage of the See Hub. Manage housing assignments, occupancy records, and key distribution. Participate in orientation, reunion activities, departmental planning, and other institutional initiatives. Collaborate with campus departments on special projects throughout the summer. Perform other duties as assigned in support of Residence Life and Student Affairs. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree required. Demonstrated leadership experience. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Strong organizational, interpersonal, and problem-solving skills. Ability to effectively supervise, mentor, and develop student leaders. Ability to exercise sound judgment, professionalism, and discretion in a fast-paced environment. Competency with technology and willingness to learn new software systems. Commitment to student development and the mission of Saint Mary's College. Ability to work evenings, weekends, holidays, overnight on-call rotations, and respond to emergencies. Ability to walk multiple flights of stairs and lift up to 25 pounds. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in Higher Education, Student Affairs, Counseling, or a related field. Experience in residence life, student affairs, or higher education. Experience with community development, student conduct, conflict resolution, advising, educational programming, and leadership development. Demonstrated commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging. Strong relationship-building skills with students from diverse backgrounds. Experience supervising student employees. Ability to build collaborative relationships across campus and contribute positively to a team-oriented environment. Schedule Full-time, 12-month, live-in position. Furnished on-campus apartment provided. Meal plan included while the dining hall is open. Comprehensive benefits package and professional development opportunities. Evening, weekend, holiday, emergency response, and overnight on-call responsibilities are required. This position is designated as essential personnel. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1229": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Admissions - Southeast US Regional Representative",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States Saint Mary's College is a nationally recognized women's Catholic college experiencing remarkable momentum, with growing enrollment, record philanthropic support, and a bold vision for the future. Description Saint Mary's College is a nationally recognized women's Catholic college experiencing remarkable momentum, with growing enrollment, record philanthropic support, and a bold vision for the future. Through innovative initiatives like the Avenue Experience, we connect every student's education to meaningful career preparation while investing in transformative experiences that extend far beyond the classroom. If you're looking to make a lasting impact alongside talented colleagues who are shaping the next generation of women leaders, Saint Mary's is an exceptional place to build your career. The Assistant Director of Admission, Southeast U.S. Regional Representative is responsible for the strategic development, management, and growth of a new and developing recruitment territory in the Southeast United States. This position requires a highly motivated, independent professional who will function as the primary face of Saint Mary's College in the region. The Representative will design and execute a comprehensive recruitment strategy, build new relationships with high schools and Community Based Organizations, and manage the full admission lifecycle for prospective students in the territory. The successful candidate will be an entrepreneurial, highly motivated, enjoy working independently, and will be able to work autonomously while maintaining a strong connection to the main campus team. He/She will represent the College at high school visits, college day/night events, open houses, and college fairs, and will actively review applications and participate in admission committee decisions. Job Responsibilities Develop and execute a comprehensive recruitment strategy for the Southeast United States recruitment territory. Build and strengthen relationships with high schools, community-based organizations, counselors, and other educational partners to expand Saint Mary's College's visibility and enrollment presence. Recruit, counsel, and guide prospective first-year and transfer students and their families throughout the admission process. Represent Saint Mary's College at high school visits, college fairs, college nights, open houses, receptions, and other recruitment events throughout the assigned territory. Manage the complete admission lifecycle, from inquiry through enrollment. Evaluate applications for admission and serve as a voting member of the Admission Committee. Collaborate with the Marketing team to develop territory-specific communication and recruitment strategies. Analyze recruitment data, monitor territory performance, and provide regular reports to the Director of Admission regarding recruitment activities, trends, and enrollment outcomes. Maintain accurate records and prospect activity using the College's CRM system. Travel periodically to Saint Mary's College for professional development, strategic planning meetings, and major campus events. Work independently to identify new recruitment opportunities, adapt strategies based on market trends, and meet or exceed enrollment goals. Collaborate closely with the Admission team and other campus partners to ensure a seamless student experience. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree. Residence within the Southeast United States, with preference given to candidates located in Georgia or North Carolina. Demonstrated ability to work independently in a remote environment with strong initiative, professionalism, and accountability. Excellent organizational, communication, interpersonal, and problem-solving skills. Experience using CRM systems and digital recruitment strategies. Proficiency with Microsoft Office, Google Workspace, and social media platforms. Experience working effectively with diverse student populations and communities. Ability to travel extensively throughout the assigned territory (approximately nine weeks annually), including evenings and weekends during peak recruitment seasons. Reliable home office with high-speed internet access. Valid driver's license and passport with the ability to travel domestically and internationally. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree. Previous experience in college admission, territory management, or regional recruitment. Familiarity with the educational landscape and college-going culture within the Southeast United States. Experience using Slate CRM. Spanish language proficiency. Demonstrated success building recruitment partnerships and growing enrollment within an assigned territory. Schedule Full-time, 12-month position based in the Southeast United States. Regular travel throughout the assigned territory is required, along with periodic travel to Saint Mary's College. Evening and weekend work is expected during peak recruitment periods. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1224": {
      "title": "Academic Affairs Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Administrative Assistant provides essential administrative, financial and operational support to four academic affairs units, that report to the Provost's Office: The Center for Academic Innovation (CFAI), Academic Research, Grants and Sponsored Programs (ARGS), the Associate Dean for Avenue Curriculum and the Spark Lab. Description The Administrative Assistant provides essential administrative, financial and operational support to four academic affairs units, that report to the Provost's Office: The Center for Academic Innovation (CFAI), Academic Research, Grants and Sponsored Programs (ARGS), the Associate Dean for Avenue Curriculum and the Spark Lab. This multi-faceted role serves as a hub for departmental coordination, ensuring efficient office operations and programming support across these diverse units. The responsibilities require efficient application of administrative and communication skills, high attention to detail, and a moderate degree of decision-making, initiative, and ability to work independently. Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent and a minimum of four (4) years of progressively responsible administrative experience, or a bachelor's degree. Minimum of three years office management or secretarial experience. Demonstrated proficiency in Microsoft Office (Word, PowerPoint, Excel) and Google Workspace (Gmail, Drive, Docs, Sheet, etc.). Working knowledge of Adobe Acrobat/PDF management. Experience with web editing and content management. Aptitude for learning and using integrated databases (Banner, PRISM, Oracle, Stellic, Dynamic Forms, Degree Works). Exceptional written and verbal communication skills; ability to serve as the public-facing representative for the departments. Forward-thinking approach to anticipating departmental needs, such as managing room reservations and event logistics. Ability to manage multiple priorities across four distinct areas and maintain rigorous records for compliance and research. High degree of discretion when handling sensitive faculty, grant, and student research data. Demonstrated ability to create newsletters, brochures, and other communications using desktop publishing software. Excellent organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines simultaneously. Ability to work independently while collaborating effectively as part of a team. Ability to build positive working relationships with faculty, staff, students, and diverse campus constituencies. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor's degree. Previous administrative experience in higher education or a similar organizational environment. Experience maintaining websites and social media platforms. Ability to foster collaborative and collegial working relationships across campus. Experience supporting faculty governance, academic programs, grants, research administration, or assessment initiatives. Job Responsibilities Administrative and Office Operations Serve as the primary point of contact by greeting and assisting visitors to the office. Provide administrative support to the Center for Academic Innovation (CFAI), the Office of Academic Research, Grants and Sponsored Programs, Academic Excellence and Inclusion, and the Provost's Office. Prepare agendas, record meeting minutes, and distribute meeting materials for various committees and departmental meetings. Create and manage brochures, emails, and monthly newsletter templates. Maintain Portal pages and digital communication platforms. Financial & Budget Administration Process check requests and stipends for faculty/student grants. Track and reconcile departmental operating and endowed budgets. Manage purchasing, bill payments, invoicing, and expense reporting. Facilitate faculty funding requests for high impact practices. Assist directors with budget monitoring and reporting. Support the administration and tracking of internal grant funding. Event and & Programming Support: Maintain departmental calendars and event schedules. Plan and coordinate professional development, workshops, symposia, and research events. Provide on-site support for event setup, breakdown, and participant communication. Process event-related invoices and maintain financial records. Student Worker Support Post positions, guide applicants through the hiring process, and lead onboarding/orientation. Manage scheduling, shift coordination, and timecard submission/approvals. Foster an inclusive culture through team building and recognition. Compliance & Record Keeping Manage grant repository and research compliance records (CITI training, IACUC, IRB). Track deadlines for research compliance and assessment records for General Education Curriculum, First Year Seminar, and Avenue Competencies. Schedule Full-time, 12-month position. Eligibility to work Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1013": {
      "title": "On-Call Blinkie Driver",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States Saint Mary’s College seeks an On-Call Blinkie Driver to work two or three days per week. Days needed may include Sundays thru Thursdays from dark to 2:00 a.m. and Fridays and Saturdays from dark to 4:00 a.m. Responsibilities include driving students to various locations around campus on a regular route. This position works during the academic year (August through May) with limited summer hours. Description Saint Mary’s College seeks an On-Call Blinkie Driver to work two or three days per week. Days needed may include Sundays thru Thursdays from dark to 2:00 a.m. and Fridays and Saturdays from dark to 4:00 a.m. Responsibilities include driving students to various locations around campus on a regular route. This position works during the academic year (August through May) with limited summer hours. The above is intended to describe the general content of and requirements for the performance of this job. It is not to be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities and requirements . Minimum Qualifications Valid driver’s license with a good driving record Strong interpersonal skills. A high school diploma or equivalent is required. Schedule On-call/ Varies Eligibility to Work: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1009": {
      "title": "On-Call Dispatcher",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The On-Call Dispatcher will coordinate and relay requests for assistance from the College community and general public to patrolling Security Officers. Description The On-Call Dispatcher will coordinate and relay requests for assistance from the College community and general public to patrolling Security Officers. The above is intended to describe the general content of and requirements for the performance of this job. It is not to be construed as an exhaustive statement of duties, responsibilities and requirements . Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or its equivalent. Proficiency in typing and data entry. No criminal record. Have a valid driver's license. Preferred Qualifications Must be able to communicate clearly on the telephone and in person. Must be able to type accurately. Eligibility to Work: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/249": {
      "title": "On-Call Safety Officer",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: IN, United States The On-Call Safety Officer is responsible for providing a safe environment for the College community and protection of College and personal property. Position Introduction The On-Call Safety Officer is responsible for providing a safe environment for the College community and protection of College and personal property. Job Description The On-Call Safety Officer will conduct preliminary investigation of incidents and accidents and files appropriate reports in accordance with established policies and procedures, and conducts patrols of the campus, locking, and securing buildings according to established policy, and responsible for guarding against unauthorized entry to campus grounds and buildings. This position will respond to medical emergencies, provides emergency first aid assistance, and transportation to medical facilities in accordance with established policies and procedures. Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or GED required. Must pass Criminal History Background check. Law Enforcement /Public Safety/Security experience preferred. Must be able to communicate clearly, both orally and in writing, on the telephone, and in person. Must be physically capable of walking, driving, and responding to emergencies in a timely manner. Must be able to work independently and possess sound reasoning capabilities. Must be physically capable of providing emergency first aid assistance. Must be able to successfully qualify in all Department training certification Must be able to interact with the campus community and public in a professional manner. Must be able to follow Chain of Command Must be able to Enforce Federal, State, Local Laws as well as College Campus Rules and regulations when dealing with College Campus Community and the Public. Must have integrity and be able to represent the Saint Mary’s College Department of Campus/Public Safety and the College in an honorable professional and impartial manner. Schedule On-Call/ Schedule Varies/ Night and Weekends Included Eligibility to Work: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/228": {
      "title": "On-Call Special Events Technician",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States Saint Mary’s College is looking for On-Call Special Event Technician. The Special Event Tech is responsible for providing technical support for College events as directed by the Technical Coordinator and the Director of Campus and Community Events. Primary support will include, load in and load out for performances/events, crew work for performances in O’Laughlin (1300 seat proscenium theatre with rigging system), Little Theatre (275 seat proscenium theatre). Serving as running crew members for events requiring lights, AV and sound on campus. Knowledge and prior experience of scenic elements for stage, lighting systems, AV systems, fly system (48 line sets), sound systems (24 channel Midas Digital console). Description Saint Mary’s College is looking for On-Call Special Event Technician. The Special Event Tech is responsible for providing technical support for College events as directed by the Technical Coordinator and the Director of Campus and Community Events. Primary support will include, load in and load out for performances/events, crew work for performances in O’Laughlin (1300 seat proscenium theatre with rigging system), Little Theatre (275 seat proscenium theatre). Serving as running crew members for events requiring lights, AV and sound on campus. Knowledge and prior experience of scenic elements for stage, lighting systems, AV systems, fly system (48-line sets), sound systems (24 channel Midas Digital console). This position will load in and load out scenery, drops, electrical cables, sound cables, props and costumes for touring productions, setting stages with sound gear, lecterns, scenic elements, lighting elements, hanging and focusing lighting instruments, running light control, AV control, and soundboards, and assisting guests with wireless mics. Minimum Qualifications Ability to push, lift, and carry heavy items (50 lbs) High School diploma Three years of either stage tech, AV, or sound experience Ability to work with a stage crew Ability to work in an organized and efficient manner Ability to provide outstanding customer service Preferred Qualifications Experience with ETC Element 2 Experience with operating a Midas sound console Experience operating a stage rigging system Schedule On-Call, Hours vary Can include weekend and evenings. Eligibility to Work: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1362": {
      "title": "Advancement Services Data Specialist",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Advancement Services Data Specialist provides administrative, operational, and data support for Saint Mary’s College Advancement Services and Alumnae Relations. Reporting to the Director of Advancement Services, this position supports the systems, processes, and workflows that sustain fundraising and alumnae engagement. Join the Saint Mary’s College Community! Saint Mary’s College, a distinguished Catholic women’s college known for its commitment to educating and empowering women, offers a welcoming and collaborative environment where meaningful work makes a difference. As part of the Advancement team, you’ll help strengthen the connections between the College, its alumnae, and its generous supporters. We are seeking a detail-oriented, service-minded professional to join our team as an Advancement Services Data Specialist. In this role, you’ll play an important part in supporting fundraising efforts, maintaining accurate donor records, and creating positive experiences for alumnae and donors. If you enjoy keeping things organized, working with data, supporting special events, and helping a team achieve its goals, we invite you to apply and become part of the Saint Mary’s College community! The Advancement Services Data Specialist provides administrative, operational, and data support for Saint Mary’s College Advancement Services and Alumnae Relations. Reporting to the Director of Advancement Services, this position supports the systems, processes, and workflows that sustain fundraising and alumnae engagement. Responsibilities include maintaining accurate constituent, alumnae, donor, and gift records; assisting with gift processing, donor acknowledgments, stewardship, and reporting; supporting alumnae communications, events, and volunteer engagement; and providing general administrative support for Advancement Services and Alumnae Relations. The successful candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to accuracy, confidentiality, customer service, and continuous process improvement. This position plays an important role in maintaining data integrity, supporting operational excellence, and strengthening relationships with donors, alumnae, volunteers, and the broader College community. Key responsibilities include: Maintaining and updating constituent, alumnae, donor, and organizational records in College database systems. Processing gifts, pledges, and charitable contributions in accordance with established policies and procedures. Supporting donor acknowledgment, receipting, stewardship, recognition, and engagement activities. Generating reports, queries, lists, and data extracts to support fundraising, events, and alumnae engagement. Assisting with data audits, record reviews, and quality-control efforts to ensure database accuracy. Preparing correspondence, reports, spreadsheets, presentations, mailings, and other administrative materials. Coordinating communications and responding professionally to routine inquiries from donors, alumnae, volunteers, and campus constituents. Providing logistical and administrative support for Reunion, alumnae events, donor events, meetings, and other Advancement Division initiatives. Assisting with event registration, attendee tracking, volunteer engagement, and constituent outreach. Maintaining departmental files, documentation, and records retention processes. Supporting operational workflows and documenting business processes, procedures, and data entry standards. Training and providing guidance to student workers as assigned. Performing other duties and special projects as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Associate’s degree or certificate in business, office administration, accounting, information management, or a related field; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Two or more years of administrative, database, customer service, advancement, nonprofit, or related office experience. Strong analytical and organizational skills, with exceptional attention to detail and a commitment to accuracy. Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise discretion when handling sensitive constituent, donor, financial, and institutional information. Proficiency with Microsoft Office and Google Workspace applications. Experience working with database systems. Strong research and problem-solving skills, including the ability to verify information and maintain accurate records. Understanding of data integrity and accurate record management. Ability to follow detailed procedures, apply established policies, and adapt processes to new situations. Ability to document data entry standards, business processes, policies, and procedures. Strong interpersonal skills, initiative, and the ability to work independently and collaboratively. Commitment to exceptional constituent service and the ability to build positive relationships with alumnae, donors, volunteers, and members of the College community. Preferred Qualifications Experience with constituent relationship management (CRM) systems and reporting tools. Experience in higher education advancement, fundraising, nonprofit operations, or alumnae relations. Experience with gift processing, donor stewardship, acknowledgment, receipting, or fundraising reports. Experience maintaining databases, conducting data audits, and supporting data quality initiatives. Experience coordinating events, managing registrations, preparing communications, or supporting volunteer engagement. Experience documenting procedures and improving administrative workflows. Experience training or supervising student workers. Schedule 40 hours per week, 12 months per year. Monday–Friday. Onsite position. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from Saint Mary’s College, either now or in the future. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1361": {
      "title": "Security Officer",
      "description": "Category: Staff JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Midnight Security Officer is responsible for providing a safe and secure environment for the College community and protecting College and personal property. This position performs a variety of public safety and security functions designed to promote the safety, well-being, and security of students, faculty, staff, visitors, and College assets. The Midnight Security Officer is responsible for providing a safe and secure environment for the College community and protecting College and personal property. This position performs a variety of public safety and security functions designed to promote the safety, well-being, and security of students, faculty, staff, visitors, and College assets. The Midnight Security Officer serves as a visible presence on campus, responds to emergencies and incidents, enforces College policies and applicable laws, provides assistance and information to the campus community, and supports a welcoming and secure environment. Successful candidates will demonstrate professionalism, integrity, sound judgment, and a commitment to serving others. Essential Responsibilities Conduct patrols of campus buildings and grounds to ensure the safety and security of the campus community and College property. Secure and monitor campus facilities by locking and unlocking buildings according to established procedures. Respond to incidents, accidents, alarms, emergencies, and requests for assistance in a timely and professional manner. Conduct preliminary investigations of incidents and accidents and complete reports in accordance with departmental policies and procedures. Provide emergency first aid assistance and arrange transportation to medical facilities when necessary. Enforce College policies, campus regulations, and applicable federal, state, and local laws. Enforce campus traffic and parking regulations and issue citations when appropriate. Serve as a resource to students, employees, visitors, and guests by providing information, directions, and assistance. Conduct and assist with fire and life safety inspections of campus buildings and grounds. Identify hazardous or unsafe conditions and submit work orders for repairs involving lights, doors, locks, and other safety-related concerns. Assist with traffic and pedestrian control during special events and campus activities. Work collaboratively with local law enforcement and emergency response agencies when necessary. Operate departmental vehicles, communication equipment, and other public safety equipment. Successfully complete and maintain required departmental certifications and training. Follow established departmental procedures and chain of command. Perform other duties as assigned by the Shift Supervisor or Chief of Campus Public Safety. Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or GED required. Ability to successfully pass a criminal history background check. Strong verbal and written communication skills. Ability to communicate effectively in person, by telephone, and in writing. Ability to work independently and exercise sound judgment in routine and emergency situations. Ability to walk, stand, drive, and respond quickly to emergencies. Ability to provide emergency first aid assistance. Ability to successfully complete and maintain all required departmental certifications and training. Ability to interact professionally and respectfully with members of the campus community and the public. Ability to enforce laws, regulations, and policies fairly and consistently. Demonstrated integrity, professionalism, and commitment to representing Saint Mary's College in an honorable and impartial manner. Preferred Qualifications Previous experience in law enforcement, public safety, campus safety, military service, security, or a related field. Experience preparing incident reports and conducting investigations. Experience working in a higher education environment. CPR, First Aid, or other emergency response certifications. Strong customer service and conflict-resolution skills. Eligibility to Work Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and must not require visa sponsorship from the College, either now or in the future. Schedule Evening, overnight, weekend, holiday, and special event coverage. "
    },
    "https://fa-eutm-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/1363": {
      "title": "Director for Student Empowerment",
      "description": "Category: Administrator JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Notre Dame, IN, United States The Director leads the Office for Student Empowerment (OSE), overseeing student advising, emergency and donor-supported funds, programming, assessment, fiscal stewardship, and community engagement. Make a Meaningful Difference in Students’ Lives At Saint Mary’s College, student success means more than academics—it means ensuring every student feels supported, connected, and empowered to reach her full potential. The Director for Student Empowerment has an opportunity to make a lasting impact by championing first-generation and limited-income students, connecting them with essential resources, and creating experiences that foster belonging, confidence, and success. Through individualized support, innovative programming, meaningful partnerships, and leadership of the Kessler Scholars Program, this role helps students navigate college with the resources and encouragement they need to thrive. The Director leads the Office for Student Empowerment (OSE), overseeing student advising, emergency and donor-supported funds, programming, assessment, fiscal stewardship, and community engagement. The Director also provides strategic direction for the Kessler Scholars Program, which offers financial, academic, professional, and personal support to help first-generation students navigate their undergraduate experience with a strong sense of belonging and purpose. This position supervises the Assistant Director for Student Empowerment and First-Generation Programs, student workers, and a summer student intern while collaborating with campus partners, alumnae, donors, and community organizations to advance student success. Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in Student Affairs, Higher Education, Education, Psychology, Sociology, or a related field. Five or more years of demonstrated successful experience in higher education, student services, or multicultural services. Effective verbal and written communication skills. Knowledge of best practices and emerging trends related to student belonging and support for first-generation and limited-income students. Ability to work effectively with diverse student populations and provide guidance on confidential and sensitive matters. Demonstrated experience implementing successful programs and strategies on a college campus. Ability to establish and maintain community partnerships and professional working relationships. Experience developing, implementing, and assessing program effectiveness. Strong administrative and organizational skills, including budget management, outcomes assessment, supervision, event planning, and follow-up. Preferred Qualifications Fluency in Spanish or another language. Schedule Full-time "
    }
  },
